00:32:23,041 --> 00:32:32,000
blew the door, went in, caught him laying on the bed with his gun at his feet,

469
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:34,880
and he was just sitting back with his hands behind his head.

470
00:32:35,500 --> 00:32:39,380
He said, well, the guy didn't have to come in like that.

471
00:32:40,880 --> 00:32:42,180
He was just in that case.

472
00:32:43,020 --> 00:32:46,100
We took him into custody with no injury to him or anybody else.

473
00:32:46,600 --> 00:32:47,300
It was fun.

474
00:32:47,780 --> 00:32:50,940
That's why I keep doing this, because to me it's fun.

475
00:32:50,941 --> 00:32:58,100
A week later, the hotel and the maid presented the LAPD SWAT team with a giant cookie

476
00:32:58,101 --> 00:33:00,860
as a token of their appreciation for their work.

477
00:33:02,860 --> 00:33:08,740
But not all perpetrators turn out to be so relaxed when confronted with the effects of their violent actions.

478
00:33:10,060 --> 00:33:16,660
If I had to make a list, number one would definitely be North Hollywood bank robbery out 97.

479
00:33:16,661 --> 00:33:21,180
Branch number 384 of the Bank of America

480
00:33:21,181 --> 00:33:24,000
sat on Laurel Canyon Boulevard in North Hollywood.

481
00:33:24,540 --> 00:33:26,980
It was February 28, 1997.

482
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:31,520
That day, started out just like another ordinary day,

483
00:33:31,620 --> 00:33:34,140
it was at the Police Academy, ready to go on a run

484
00:33:34,141 --> 00:33:35,620
with a couple other guys,

485
00:33:36,580 --> 00:33:39,060
and one of the SWAT officers, Donnie Anderson,

486
00:33:39,360 --> 00:33:41,160
came driving up and said that there was a robbery,

487
00:33:41,380 --> 00:33:44,040
bank robbery, suspects were armed with automatic weapons,

488
00:33:44,041 --> 00:33:45,660
and there was officers down at the scene.

489
00:33:45,661 --> 00:33:53,860
Anticipating possible police response, the perpetrators were heavily armed and covered from neck to toe in body armor.

490
00:33:53,861 --> 00:33:59,160
For over 30 minutes, they fired shots from their weapons with complete abandon.

491
00:33:59,161 --> 00:34:10,101
So when they exited the bank, the patrol officers were there, they challenged them verbally and immediately they were met with literally hundreds of rounds of

492
00:34:10,102 --> 00:34:15,640
automatic weapons fire, semi-automatic weapons fire with armor, piercing rounds.

493
00:34:15,660 --> 00:34:16,660
RAPS.

494
00:34:16,661 --> 00:34:25,660
Racing through rush hour traffic, SWAT team members arrived unseen some 31 minutes after the call went out.

495
00:34:25,661 --> 00:34:30,660
It's one of those incidents where you get unseen and it's just absolute chaos.

496
00:34:30,661 --> 00:34:38,660
The noise of people screaming, the noise of people going to ground and hiding, and the helicopters.

497
00:34:38,661 --> 00:34:44,660
And in the backdrop of all this chaos is automatic gunfire from high-powered rifles.

498
00:34:44,661 --> 00:34:47,660
It was obvious that time was of the essence.

499
00:34:47,661 --> 00:34:50,660
The sounds of gunfire continued.

500
00:34:50,661 --> 00:34:53,660
To hear it that loud and that consistently just

501
00:34:53,661 --> 00:34:55,660
boom, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom,

502
00:34:55,661 --> 00:34:57,660
ongoing, not stopping.

503
00:34:57,661 --> 00:35:00,660
It, yeah, it kind of makes the hair

504
00:35:00,661 --> 00:35:02,660
in the back of your head stand up a little bit.

505
00:35:02,661 --> 00:35:07,660
As the shooting continued and patrol officers contained the perpetrators,

506
00:35:07,661 --> 00:35:09,660
SWAT team members got in their car

507
00:35:09,661 --> 00:35:12,660
and headed toward the sound of gunfire.

508
00:35:12,661 --> 00:35:13,660
One of them was SWAT veterans,

509
00:35:13,661 --> 00:35:15,660
was SWAT veteran Stephen Gomez.

510
00:35:16,660 --> 00:35:22,420
I could see a pickup truck and a small compact vehicle parked next to it.

511
00:35:22,540 --> 00:35:24,660
The small compact had its trunk up.

512
00:35:25,420 --> 00:35:27,360
You could see an individual moving around,

513
00:35:28,220 --> 00:35:32,520
and as we got closer, you could see bullet holes in the windshield of the pickup truck,

514
00:35:33,120 --> 00:35:37,040
and we see that the driver in the pickup truck was trying to drive away

515
00:35:37,041 --> 00:35:37,820
is the suspect.

516
00:35:37,940 --> 00:35:43,640
He's got a ski mask on, and he's got an M-16 kind of holding him at a part,

517
00:35:43,660 --> 00:35:45,960
forward arms position while he's trying to start the pickup truck.

518
00:35:46,760 --> 00:35:52,480
All the hours of training paid off, resulting in effective, near-automatic responses.

519
00:35:53,620 --> 00:35:57,320
I leaned out of the window, turned, and then I put several rounds into the windshield

520
00:35:57,321 --> 00:35:58,880
and tried to stop the suspect.

521
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:03,760
He rolled out of the pickup truck and ran to the back of that small compact car.

522
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:09,040
The suspect continued to fire wildly with his heavy weapons arsenal.

523
00:36:10,220 --> 00:36:11,880
It was like all heck broke loose.

524
00:36:11,980 --> 00:36:13,640
It was constant automatic fire.

525
00:36:13,660 --> 00:36:22,540
And I was, as I was crawling across that seat, which probably only took, probably two, three seconds to get out of that car.

526
00:36:22,580 --> 00:36:24,700
But it seemed like eternity because I could hear his firing.

527
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:30,200
And the first thought in my mind was, the very first thought was, I said, Lord, please look out for us.

528
00:36:30,640 --> 00:36:37,340
The three SWAT officers ended the robbery and shooting spree by shooting and killing the last perpetrator.

529
00:36:38,660 --> 00:36:43,560
Dement kicks in and all those things that your body goes or those changes when you're ending that fight or flight syndrome.

530
00:36:43,660 --> 00:36:49,340
I'll kick in and I'm here to tell you everything, everything kicked in that day.

531
00:36:50,420 --> 00:36:56,660
Ten patrol officers had been wounded, along with six civilians, but only the perpetrators were killed.

532
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:02,360
The effectiveness and the necessity of the LAPD SWAT team was proven once again.

533
00:37:03,820 --> 00:37:10,260
The North Hollywood shooting was what SWAT's original intent was, and that was to respond to an incident

534
00:37:10,261 --> 00:37:15,080
when patrol officers were doing as good a job as they possibly could,

535
00:37:15,580 --> 00:37:17,640
but they were not capable of ending it.

536
00:37:18,400 --> 00:37:20,940
And that was the real reason SWAT was formed.

537
00:37:21,980 --> 00:37:27,060
Like public servants everywhere, Sergeant Gomez is unwilling to accept anything

538
00:37:27,061 --> 00:37:29,800
other than the recognition that he performed his duty.

539
00:37:31,540 --> 00:37:33,880
People say, you guys are heroism.

540
00:37:34,020 --> 00:37:35,000
I said, no, no, we're not heroes.

541
00:37:35,060 --> 00:37:38,560
We were just three experienced officers with a lot of training

542
00:37:38,561 --> 00:37:40,240
that did their job that day.

543
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:49,020
Now that's what confrontation and aggression in a measured and controlled and well-trained way creates.

544
00:37:49,760 --> 00:37:53,000
It creates success on the part of the police.

545
00:37:53,700 --> 00:38:05,340
With skills refined and honed by training and years of successful call-ups, LAPD's SWAT team continues to demonstrate the value and the importance of SWAT.

546
00:38:05,341 --> 00:38:16,640
The LAPD trains for all types of scenarios, utilizing past incidents, and contemplating various possibilities that may occur in the future.

547
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:24,660
In my mind, you never become an expert, because as soon as you start thinking you're an expert in SWAT tactics,

548
00:38:24,900 --> 00:38:31,180
and you know everything there is to know about tactics, something is going to bite them that you've never encountered before.

549
00:38:31,181 --> 00:38:36,340
So you constantly had to be trying to learn new ways of doing things

550
00:38:36,341 --> 00:38:39,260
and constantly be trying to improve yourselves.

551
00:38:40,620 --> 00:38:43,160
In addition to training and experience,

552
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:47,600
another vital reason for the success of the LAPD's SWAT team

553
00:38:47,601 --> 00:38:49,300
is the equipment they use.

554
00:38:49,860 --> 00:38:53,900
Many of the weapons are modified by their full-time armor, Jim Moody.

555
00:38:54,540 --> 00:38:58,700
The officers are issued a primary set of weapons,

556
00:38:59,180 --> 00:39:01,140
pistols, rifles, shotgun.

557
00:39:01,180 --> 00:39:05,180
These are all the extra types of equipment they carry with them.

558
00:39:05,181 --> 00:39:10,180
These weapons up here are the 37 millimeter gas guns with six shots.

559
00:39:10,181 --> 00:39:14,180
They'll also come and they look like this for a single shot.

560
00:39:14,181 --> 00:39:18,180
While tear gas has been a staple of the SWAT arsenal from the beginning,

561
00:39:18,181 --> 00:39:23,180
in recent years, law enforcement has been able to utilize new, less lethal options,

562
00:39:23,181 --> 00:39:28,180
including rubber bullets, bean bags, and special crowd dispersal rounds.

563
00:39:28,181 --> 00:39:35,180
Another new piece of equipment is the SWAT command and control vehicle.

564
00:39:35,181 --> 00:39:42,180
The acquisition in the aftermath of the North Hollywood incident was in part due to the rigorous post-incident review process

565
00:39:42,181 --> 00:39:47,180
that's been part of LAPD SWAT since the beginning.

566
00:39:47,181 --> 00:39:53,180
Whenever possible, the vehicle is staffed with psychologists who aid in negotiation with suspects.

567
00:39:53,181 --> 00:39:57,180
But unlike many other SWAT teams, the negotiators are actual SWAT team matters.

568
00:39:57,181 --> 00:40:00,180
SWAT team members, not a separate group.

569
00:40:00,181 --> 00:40:04,180
We have 60 police officers,

570
00:40:04,181 --> 00:40:07,180
21 of the 60, also have a collateral duty

571
00:40:07,181 --> 00:40:10,180
of being a primary negotiator, a communicator.

572
00:40:10,181 --> 00:40:12,180
All 60 go through the negotiation training,

573
00:40:12,181 --> 00:40:16,180
so they have a reference as far as communicating with these folks.

574
00:40:16,181 --> 00:40:20,180
And we have found it to be very, very successful.

575
00:40:20,181 --> 00:40:24,180
When the SWAT team is not training and not on call-up,

576
00:40:24,181 --> 00:40:26,180
they're utilized to patrol high crime areas.

577
00:40:26,181 --> 00:40:31,940
areas, there's very little downtime for SWAT team members.

578
00:40:31,941 --> 00:40:36,780
While the nature of incidents that require the SWAT team haven't changed in recent years,

579
00:40:36,781 --> 00:40:41,540
there are several situations that require law enforcement to think in a new way.

580
00:40:41,541 --> 00:40:46,000
One is when there are active shooters, as in Columbine High School.

581
00:40:46,001 --> 00:40:55,400
Since the April 1999 incident, SWAT teams are more prepared to take aggressive countermeasures.

582
00:40:55,401 --> 00:41:01,300
This is a change in tactics from the control, contain, negotiate approach to an active approach

583
00:41:01,301 --> 00:41:04,280
which calls for rapidly engaging lethal suspects.

584
00:41:07,240 --> 00:41:11,420
Columbine was a big wake-up call, but there had been other situations around the country,

585
00:41:11,421 --> 00:41:14,400
but those were not perceived as wake-up calls in the law enforcement community.

586
00:41:14,401 --> 00:41:19,080
We view the school as a sacred place of protection for our kids, and when that sacred envelope

587
00:41:19,081 --> 00:41:23,800
was punctured, that's when we started saying, you know what, we need to get law enforcement

588
00:41:23,801 --> 00:41:26,180
to go and go quick and go big.

589
00:41:26,820 --> 00:41:28,720
Columbine was not the first of the school shootings.

590
00:41:28,840 --> 00:41:29,880
There had been several before,

591
00:41:30,340 --> 00:41:32,400
but this was the one where the static model

592
00:41:32,401 --> 00:41:34,600
of law enforcement response was employed,

593
00:41:34,720 --> 00:41:35,520
and it didn't work.

594
00:41:36,920 --> 00:41:40,080
The new approach in these situations differs from the old,

595
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:42,800
but the difficult decision remains the same.

596
00:41:43,320 --> 00:41:45,240
When to wait, when to go.

597
00:41:46,120 --> 00:41:48,200
The question I always ask myself

598
00:41:48,201 --> 00:41:50,540
if there's going to be any type of tactical intervention

599
00:41:50,541 --> 00:41:51,320
is why now?

600
00:41:51,321 --> 00:41:58,920
Why now versus 20 minutes ago or 30 minutes ago, or why now versus 30 minutes from now, 40 minutes from now?

601
00:41:59,380 --> 00:42:06,580
This is a critical issue that people have to understand that if law enforcement goes in, it could be bloody.

602
00:42:06,880 --> 00:42:16,812
However, the flip side is that if we sit around on our butt and wait and don't go in, then the terrorist or the person who is crazed has free reign to continue on

603
00:42:16,813 --> 00:42:18,100
their path of murder.

604
00:42:18,101 --> 00:42:29,041
This new acceptance of the necessity for greater risk in times of greater danger is part of the new landscape of America in the wake of the terrorist attacks of

605
00:42:29,042 --> 00:42:30,340
September 11, 2001.

606
00:42:31,600 --> 00:42:41,304
The law enforcement element of our society has to be equipped to respond to these things, and the only way they're going to keep up is through their own continuous

607
00:42:41,305 --> 00:42:42,005
research.

608
00:42:42,006 --> 00:42:48,080
And SWAT officers tend to be real good at conducting.

609
00:42:48,100 --> 00:42:53,100
conducting research in technology and techniques as well as tactics.

610
00:42:53,101 --> 00:42:54,100
Clear.

611
00:42:54,101 --> 00:42:57,100
And yet the dangers some think are growing.

612
00:42:57,101 --> 00:43:01,100
I would say the risk facing SWAT isn't beat on the rise

613
00:43:01,101 --> 00:43:04,100
because it's only when you run up against the committed crooks,

614
00:43:04,101 --> 00:43:09,100
like the guys in North Hollywood, or the terrorists who are willing to die for what they believe,

615
00:43:09,101 --> 00:43:14,100
that you can see that police are at a disadvantage,

616
00:43:14,101 --> 00:43:16,100
and SWAT can get at a disadvantage.

617
00:43:16,101 --> 00:43:17,100
By definition, it becomes more dangerous.

618
00:43:17,101 --> 00:43:18,100
it becomes more dangerous.

619
00:43:18,101 --> 00:43:20,100
While the risks may be growing,

620
00:43:20,101 --> 00:43:26,100
the resolve among SWAT team members to do whatever it takes to end the problem runs deep.

621
00:43:26,101 --> 00:43:29,100
The people of SWAT know there can be only one outcome,

622
00:43:29,101 --> 00:43:36,100
and making that happen takes patience, intelligence, commitment, and courage.

623
00:43:36,101 --> 00:43:42,100
Sometimes there's only one way to take violent offenders into custody

624
00:43:42,101 --> 00:43:46,100
if they're going to resist violently, and that's with violence.

625
00:43:46,101 --> 00:43:48,060
It can't be done any other way.

626
00:43:48,160 --> 00:43:49,720
There is no magical solution.

627
00:43:50,560 --> 00:43:52,460
And I doubt that there ever will be.

628
00:43:53,440 --> 00:43:57,300
But whatever they may face, the men and women of SWAT everywhere

629
00:43:57,301 --> 00:44:00,240
share the same goal as other law enforcement officers

630
00:44:00,241 --> 00:44:05,340
to protect and serve, no matter what the risk, no matter what the cost.

631
00:44:06,400 --> 00:44:12,560
Their job is to go out and save people's lives, whether they be hostages, whether they be the bad guys.

632
00:44:12,561 --> 00:44:19,980
And if you look at SWAT columns today, you'll find that most of them are resolved in a peaceful way.

633
00:44:20,520 --> 00:44:25,220
So when people remind me of we are peace officers, that's exactly what SWAT is.

634
00:44:25,600 --> 00:44:26,520
They are peace officers.

635
00:44:26,720 --> 00:44:30,280
They are bringing peace to a situation, a very violent situation.

